pg_bp_lirs -- LIRS-2 (Low Inter-reference Recency Set)
======================================================

pg_bp_lirs provides the LIRS-2 buffer replacement algorithm as a
PostgreSQL extension.  LIRS-2 can be used with dynamic buffer pools:

    CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_lirs;
    CREATE BUFFER POOL my_lirs_pool HANDLER lirs_pool_handler SIZE '33554432';
    CREATE TABLE hot_data (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_lirs_pool');


What Is LIRS-2?
===============

LIRS (Low Inter-reference Recency Set) is a cache replacement algorithm
that tracks the Inter-Reference Recency (IRR) of each page -- the number
of distinct pages accessed between two consecutive accesses to the same
page.  Pages with low IRR are classified as "hot" (LIR status) and kept
in cache.  Pages with high IRR are classified as "cold" (HIR status) and
become eviction candidates.

Unlike ARC and CAR, which partition the cache into recency and frequency
lists, LIRS directly measures the reuse distance of each page.  This
makes LIRS particularly effective at handling cyclic and looping access
patterns where the loop size varies.

LIRS-2 is an improvement over the original LIRS algorithm (SIGMETRICS
2002) that bounds the internal stack data structure to 2x the cache size.
The original LIRS had unbounded stack growth proportional to the total
number of distinct pages ever accessed, which made it impractical for
long-running workloads.  LIRS-2 solves this by pruning non-LIR entries
from the stack bottom when the bound is exceeded.


When LIRS-2 Excels
===================

LIRS-2 is the best choice for workloads with variable reuse distances:

  - Cyclic/looping access patterns where the loop size exceeds the
    cache but individual pages are revisited at predictable intervals.
    Example: a query repeatedly scanning the same set of tables in a
    fixed order, where the total working set slightly exceeds the pool.

  - Mixed reuse distances where some pages are accessed every few
    hundred operations and others every few thousand.  LIRS keeps the
    short-distance pages hot regardless of how recently they were last
    touched, while ARC/CAR may evict them after a burst of unrelated
    accesses.

  - Workloads where ARC/CAR thrash because they cannot distinguish
    "will be reused at a known distance" from "random one-shot access."


When to Prefer ARC or CAR Instead
==================================

  - High-concurrency OLTP with mostly random point lookups: all three
    algorithms degrade similarly under purely random access, but CAR's
    atomic reference bit has lower per-hit overhead than LIRS's spinlock.

  - Memory-constrained systems: LIRS requires 3 CDB entries per buffer
    (roughly 200+ bytes per buffer) versus 2 CDB entries for ARC/CAR.
    For very large pools this difference adds up.

  - Workloads dominated by sequential scans with rare re-access: ARC's
    adaptive T1/T2 balance and CAR's clock sweep handle this well with
    less overhead than LIRS's stack maintenance.


Comparison with ARC and CAR
============================

    Aspect              ARC                 CAR                 LIRS-2
    ------              ---                 ---                 ------
    Hit tracking        Spinlock + list     Atomic ref bit      Spinlock + list
    Adaptation          Ghost T1/T2 size   Ghost T1/T2 size    IRR-based LIR/HIR
    Scan resistance     Ghost-based         Ghost-based         IRR tracking
    Cyclic workloads    May thrash          May thrash          Handles well
    Eviction target     T1/T2 LRU          Clock hand sweep    Q head (HIR)
    Prefetch hint       Not supported       Ghost-aware         Ghost-aware
    Bgwriter hint       None                T1 clock hand       Q head position
    CDB per buffer      2x                  2x                  3x
    Per-hit lock        Spinlock            None (atomic)       Spinlock


VACUUM Integration
==================

VACUUM-loaded pages are inserted at Q head (the eviction end) without
being pushed onto the LIRS stack.  This makes them the first candidates
for eviction, preventing VACUUM operations from polluting the cache with
transient pages.  Because these pages have no stack membership, they
cannot be promoted to LIR status.


Ghost-Aware Prefetch
====================

LIRS implements the prefetch_hint callback.  When the buffer manager
signals upcoming page accesses, LIRS checks the ghost hash table.
Ghost entries found are moved to the stack top, preventing premature
recycling during stack pruning.  This pre-warms the IRR tracking so
the ghost entry is correctly positioned for LIR promotion when the
actual page access arrives.


Q-Head-Priority Trickle Writer
===============================

LirsSyncStart returns the Q head position as the trickle writer's
starting point.  HIR pages at Q head are the most likely eviction
candidates, so writing them to disk proactively reduces eviction
latency when a backend later needs a free buffer.


Statistics
==========

    SELECT * FROM pg_stat_lirs;

Columns:

    name             -- buffer pool name
    oid              -- buffer pool OID
    lir_size         -- current number of resident LIR (hot) pages
    hir_size         -- current number of resident HIR (cold) pages
    ghost_size       -- current number of non-resident ghost entries
    lir_capacity     -- maximum LIR pages allowed (set at init)
    stack_size       -- current stack S size (bounded to 2x cache)
    q_size           -- current Q list size (resident HIR count)
    lookups          -- total page lookups
    lir_hits         -- hits on LIR (hot) pages
    hir_hits         -- hits on HIR (cold) pages
    ghost_hits       -- hits on ghost (non-resident) entries
    misses           -- complete cache misses
    lir_demotions    -- LIR pages demoted to HIR
    hir_promotions   -- HIR/ghost pages promoted to LIR
    evictions        -- pages evicted from cache
    stack_prunes     -- LIRS-2 stack pruning events


Size Advisory
-------------

    SELECT * FROM pg_bp_lirs_size_recommendation('my_lirs_pool')
        AS (current_size int, recommended_size int,
            ghost_pressure float8, hit_ratio float8);

This function examines the ratio of ghost hits to total misses (ghost
pressure).  High ghost pressure (above 0.1) indicates that recently
evicted pages are being re-accessed, suggesting the pool would benefit
from more buffers.  The recommended_size scales linearly with ghost
pressure up to 2x the current size.


Limitations
===========

Spinlock on every hit.  Like ARC, LIRS requires a spinlock acquisition
for every cache hit to maintain stack ordering.  CAR's atomic reference
bit approach has lower per-hit overhead.  Under high concurrency this
can become a bottleneck.

3x CDB memory.  LIRS needs 3 Cache Directory Block entries per physical
buffer (versus 2x for ARC/CAR) to accommodate ghost entries within the
bounded stack.  Each LirsCDB is roughly 64 bytes, bringing the total
metadata overhead to roughly 200+ bytes per buffer.

Stack pruning overhead.  The LIRS-2 stack pruning can be bursty: a
single access may trigger removal of many non-LIR entries from the
stack bottom.  This is bounded by the 2x cache_size limit but can
cause latency spikes under certain access patterns.

Fixed LIR capacity ratio.  The LIR/HIR partition is set at pool
initialization time based on pool size (75% LIR for pools under 128
buffers, scaling up to 99% LIR for pools of 8192+ buffers) and does
not adapt dynamically at runtime.  This differs from ARC, where
target_T1_size adjusts continuously based on ghost hit patterns.


References
==========

S. Jiang and X. Zhang, "LIRS: An Efficient Low Inter-reference
Recency Set Replacement Policy to Improve Buffer Cache Performance",
Proceedings of ACM SIGMETRICS 2002.
https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/511399.511340

X. Jiang et al., "LIRS2: An Improved LIRS Replacement Algorithm",
HPL-2017-28, 2017.
